00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:04.140
So now here are the
questions that I got, uh...

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:09.200
says Gujarati has been awesome
to study, blah, blah, blah.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:11.030
When the joy dwindles I switch to French.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:16.429
So, uh, The point here is that
we have Gujarati at LingQ.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:18.419
I don't know how many
people are studying it.

00:01:18.509 --> 00:01:21.690
Uh, I'm not, I may, one day it's
not the major Indian language,

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.500
but someone volunteered to do
the mini stories and that's our

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.419
condition for adding a new language.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.609
It's also interesting that, uh, having
a major and a minor language sometimes

00:01:32.609 --> 00:01:35.680
can be good for maintaining interest.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:41.260
I'm struggling a bit right now because I
have my Persian and my Arabic, and I've

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:44.350
even started into a Egyptian Arabic.

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:48.040
So I've got three sort of languages
bouncing around and there was some

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:53.650
suggestion that I might do a video, uh,
with a Russian person who lives in Turkey.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:55.960
And so I went back and
tried to refresh my Turkish.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:00.070
So all these languages bouncing
around in your brain is an obstacle.

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:05.460
It is confusing, but it also is this
element of things new and interesting.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:08.370
And, uh, you know, I'm a debutante.

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:09.449
So I enjoy doing that.

00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:10.430
I think, uh...

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:12.420
is doing it for fun.

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:17.700
Obviously, if you're going to go for
IELTS or something, uh, TOEIC, then you

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.370
really want to focus on one language.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:20.840
All right.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:25.620
Next person, Capacitor says the problem
is that when you reach an advanced

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.630
level, it becomes more difficult to learn
more because you already know so much.

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:32.730
And thus it becomes more difficult to
stay motivated with the learning process.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:40.470
I mean, I've said it many times, the word
frequency declines very, very steeply.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:46.320
So the more words you learn, the more
difficult it is to learn new words,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:51.210
because these new words will appear
less and less frequently in whatever

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:52.769
you're listening to or reading.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.490
So, you know, I've sometimes said
that when we start in a language,

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:59.880
we have that initial, I talk about
the upside down hockey stick.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.830
We have that initial period: wow, we're
learning a lot of words because the most

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:08.420
frequent words show up so often and all
of a sudden we understand some things.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:13.100
And, uh, you know, we can even say a
few things, but then we discovered that

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:17.300
there's actually a long road before
you reach sort of a decent level of

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:22.770
fluency, because unfortunately we need
a lot of words to understand books, to

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:27.210
understand movies, to even understand
conversations that native speakers are

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:31.020
having because native speakers actually
have a lot of words because they've been

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.940
with that language since they were kids.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:36.330
And that they say as a rule of
thumb, that you add a thousand

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.770
words for every year of your life.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:40.710
So someone who's 40 years
old has 40,000 words.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:45.330
As a learner to acquire all these
words, it takes a long time.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:50.670
The secret therefore is to not be
so conscious of the words you're

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:53.500
acquiring and forgetting and
reacquiring and re-forgetting.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:58.500
But if you can find content of interest
that you are motivated by learning

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:03.045
about the country or learning about
some aspect of the country, Some subject

00:04:03.045 --> 00:04:06.405
that may not even be related to the
country, but it's a subject of interest

00:04:06.405 --> 00:04:09.855
to you and you're learning it in the
language that you're trying to learn.

00:04:10.095 --> 00:04:14.655
So now your focus shifts from sort of
deliberately learning the language to,

00:04:15.225 --> 00:04:21.375
you know, consuming a lot of interesting,
compelling content in that language.

00:04:22.125 --> 00:04:25.815
And I did say earlier that it's difficult
to start with compelling content, but when

00:04:25.815 --> 00:04:29.715
you reach the level of where you already
know a lot of words, the content has to

00:04:29.715 --> 00:04:34.455
be compelling or it's hard to continue,
even though, as I also said, I sometimes

00:04:34.455 --> 00:04:38.294
go back to my mini stories, especially
if I'm preparing to speak and to use the

00:04:38.294 --> 00:04:40.635
language, because that helps me to train.

00:04:40.635 --> 00:04:45.195
It's kind of like my gym, it's my core
exercises, but yeah, you need that

00:04:45.255 --> 00:04:47.054
compelling input in order to keep going.

00:04:47.054 --> 00:04:47.594
For sure.

00:04:48.604 --> 00:04:51.114
Uh, ... says I have a question for
people who are learning the  language

00:04:51.114 --> 00:04:54.915
and living in the country where
the target language is spoken.

00:04:55.695 --> 00:04:57.465
So I've been learning English,
I don't have a chance to use

00:04:57.465 --> 00:04:58.665
spoken English currently.

00:04:58.665 --> 00:05:00.105
I'm going to go to Germany.

00:05:00.135 --> 00:05:02.535
My level is A1, I'm wondering
whether their learning will be

00:05:02.535 --> 00:05:05.655
easier and faster because of the
environment of German native speakers.

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:06.305
All right.

00:05:07.325 --> 00:05:07.985
A warning.

00:05:08.165 --> 00:05:11.885
I have always found that when you go
to that country where the language is

00:05:11.885 --> 00:05:19.745
spoken, you have to have a level of B1,
B almost B2 in order to take advantage.

00:05:20.685 --> 00:05:24.825
Uh, because not everyone you deal with
in a shop or on the street, and of

00:05:24.825 --> 00:05:29.094
course this depends on the level of
your language or a language that you

00:05:29.094 --> 00:05:31.484
know, what that level is in the country.

00:05:31.484 --> 00:05:36.135
So if you go to Germany where the level
of English is quite good, uh, the average

00:05:36.164 --> 00:05:40.315
person you encounter will have a level
of English that is higher than A1.

00:05:40.844 --> 00:05:45.734
So you either, either you have to find
people who have practically no, uh,

00:05:45.945 --> 00:05:50.670
English, and they will then humor you
in German, or you have to get a teacher.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:56.040
So my advice always is to do as
much as you can before you go.

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:59.220
And even when you are in the
country, you have to work

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.290
hard on your input activities.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:06.270
So when I lived in Japan, I did a lot
of listening and reading, this is course

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:12.420
45 years ago, on my own because the
amount that I was able to use, you know,

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:14.490
in the early stages was very limited.

00:06:15.224 --> 00:06:19.065
So you can't rely on being
surrounded by the, by the language.

00:06:19.065 --> 00:06:21.885
Of course you turn on the TV,
you can listen to the radio, you

00:06:21.885 --> 00:06:24.315
can read newspapers, all of that,
which you can do in your home

00:06:24.315 --> 00:06:26.445
country, too, in whatever language.

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:30.600
But the more you can build
it up before you get there.

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:35.490
And the more you continue your input
activities so that you can get yourself

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:40.260
up to almost a, I would say B1 and a
half in terms of your comprehension,

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:41.430
the better you're going to do.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:44.550
And the more likely people will
speak to you in the language you are

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:45.770
learning, in the target language.

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.290
So Mac says, uh, what do I have
to do in order to take IELTS

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:55.880
or TOEFL, if I'm learning on my
own, what kind of techniques?

00:06:56.730 --> 00:07:01.770
So many people need these,
uh, tests of English.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:05.910
Like IELTS, TOEFL, they need
them to get into a good school

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:07.030
or they need them for work.

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:13.355
My feeling is that you can get copies
of those tests so that you have some

00:07:13.355 --> 00:07:15.155
sense of what the tests are all about.

00:07:15.275 --> 00:07:18.395
You can even do, you know, example tests.

00:07:18.425 --> 00:07:22.985
All of this material is available on the
internet, but fundamentally you have to

00:07:23.045 --> 00:07:26.255
raise up your overall language skills.

00:07:26.435 --> 00:07:28.205
You have to become a better reader.

00:07:28.205 --> 00:07:32.175
You have to read fast, or you're
going to run out of time in the test.

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:34.710
So again, it comes back
to listening and reading.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:40.010
Personally, I think LingQ is a great
way to work on TOEIC or IELTS because

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:45.980
it's, it's all about comprehension,
vocabulary, and you listen to so much

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:47.630
and you read so much that you have...

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:52.859
you get a better sense of what's
correct grammar and not correct grammar.

00:07:52.859 --> 00:07:55.080
You don't have to scratch
your brain as much.

00:07:55.349 --> 00:07:59.039
And many of those questions
on those tests, comprehension

00:07:59.039 --> 00:08:03.090
questions, or questions about what
is correct and not correct usage.

00:08:03.479 --> 00:08:08.460
If you, if you can read quickly and if
you can understand well for the parts

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:12.570
where you have to listen to content, the
better you comprehend, the better you

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:15.044
understand, the better you're going to do.

00:08:15.135 --> 00:08:18.075
The better your'll feel for the
language, the better you're going to do.

00:08:18.435 --> 00:08:22.484
I don't think it's that useful,
uh, to study, you know, the 5,000

00:08:22.484 --> 00:08:24.195
words that will show up in TOEIC.

00:08:24.445 --> 00:08:27.645
You can do that, and there's nothing
wrong with doing that, but it should

00:08:27.645 --> 00:08:32.954
be done against the backdrop, backdrop
of having elevated your overall

00:08:32.954 --> 00:08:36.855
level in the language, listening,
comprehension, reading skills, vocabulary.

00:08:38.745 --> 00:08:40.125
Uh, okay.

00:08:40.455 --> 00:08:44.325
What about reading the dictionary
from page, from the front to the back?

00:08:44.565 --> 00:08:49.605
It's not something that I would do, uh,
you know, it's one of these activities,

00:08:49.605 --> 00:08:51.795
it's like studying a list of words.

00:08:52.295 --> 00:08:58.275
Um, you know, you think you're doing
something, you think you're learning.

00:08:58.275 --> 00:09:01.485
I don't think you're learning very
much, so it can be interesting.

00:09:01.485 --> 00:09:05.655
And if you see a lot of words that you
already kind of have no idea, Sort of

00:09:05.655 --> 00:09:11.715
reinforce what you are still trying to
learn, but I don't think it's a tremendous

00:09:11.715 --> 00:09:16.755
strategy and it's hard to keep doing,
but any exposure to the language is good.

00:09:20.625 --> 00:09:24.170
Now, here's a question...

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:26.010
Says, is finding out...

00:09:26.939 --> 00:09:30.200
he learns the conjugations in Spanish,
but how are they used, you know?...

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:34.260
in other words, the different pass forms.

00:09:34.319 --> 00:09:35.829
Well, this is true in any language.

00:09:35.829 --> 00:09:40.670
In English when do we
say I, I have done it.

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:41.170
I did it.

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:47.255
Simple past versus a, the sort of
whatever it's called in English...

00:09:47.695 --> 00:09:53.585
Uh, these are things that have to do
with usage, and it's not just when you

00:09:53.585 --> 00:09:57.635
use these different conjugation forms,
it's even a matter of which words are

00:09:57.635 --> 00:09:59.605
used together with which other words.

00:09:59.825 --> 00:10:01.890
Word usage, word usage.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:07.140
These are, this is a matter of
developing habits in the language.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:11.190
And I believe that a lot of listening
and reading, hearing the language

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:13.650
starts to create patterns in your brain.

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:18.690
So certain forms, certain conjugation
forms are going to seem more natural.

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:25.440
There are always sort of trigger
words that trigger the tense because

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:28.500
we're here talking about tenses in
the case of this conjugation forum.

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:31.965
And so, you know, Just now I did...

00:10:32.205 --> 00:10:32.475
okay.

00:10:32.475 --> 00:10:38.175
"Just now" suggests simple
past, uh, while, uh, "while I

00:10:38.175 --> 00:10:39.795
was watching TV, I was also..."

00:10:39.795 --> 00:10:43.814
so the "while" tends to suggest a more,
a continuous form, sort of the imperfect.

00:10:43.935 --> 00:10:48.915
So you should look at words that
surround these different tenses, whether

00:10:48.915 --> 00:10:52.469
it's in Spanish or English or any
other languages, and you will see that

00:10:52.589 --> 00:10:56.609
certain tenses are suggested by certain
trigger words, and you should notice

00:10:56.609 --> 00:10:58.619
those and maybe save them at LingQ.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:00.510
And all of this helps you notice.

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:05.010
And once you notice things, you can
develop the proper language habits so

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:06.869
that the usage will seem more natural.

00:11:06.959 --> 00:11:08.760
That doesn't mean you'll
always get it right.

00:11:09.329 --> 00:11:11.849
And in many cases, it may
not even matter what you use.

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:16.530
Uh, in English sometimes it's
just best to use the simple past.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:18.210
Can't go wrong kind of thing.

00:11:18.689 --> 00:11:22.860
Uh, but you want to develop a feel
and that's only achieved through

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:26.040
lots of listening and reading and
eventually using the language.

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:28.220
Um...

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:34.469
starting in Mandarin the same
as everything else, except you

00:11:34.469 --> 00:11:35.579
have to learn the characters.

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:37.770
What do you think about
negative motivation?

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:41.130
Like if people aren't nice to you,
I, you know, he goes on, uh, make

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:42.390
fun of immigrant students and stuff.

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:44.270
I mean, you have to turn that off.

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:47.060
Like there are going to be
nasty people in this world.

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:50.150
There are people who will make
fun of you in any situation, not

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:51.440
just having to do with language.

00:11:51.704 --> 00:11:54.495
And you can't take these
things too seriously.

00:11:54.755 --> 00:11:56.415
The majority of people are nice.

00:11:56.415 --> 00:11:58.095
The majority of people are encouraging.

00:11:58.214 --> 00:11:59.295
Some people are not.

00:11:59.505 --> 00:12:02.025
And I just turn off the people who are...

00:12:02.265 --> 00:12:04.155
I try to have a very selective memory.

00:12:04.574 --> 00:12:06.704
When I was in Japan
everyone was nice to me.

00:12:06.704 --> 00:12:08.385
That doesn't mean everyone was nice to me.

00:12:08.385 --> 00:12:11.324
That means I only remember the
people who were nice to me.

00:12:11.415 --> 00:12:13.785
And I think you have to
focus on the positive.

00:12:15.365 --> 00:12:17.915
Um, you talked about reading,
reading, reading, but do you

00:12:17.915 --> 00:12:20.405
do this silently or out loud?

00:12:20.795 --> 00:12:21.295
Okay.

00:12:22.095 --> 00:12:27.795
Reading I read, I think I'm reading
silently and I'll often depending

00:12:27.795 --> 00:12:30.495
on where I am in the language,
I will listen while I'm reading.

00:12:31.439 --> 00:12:36.390
I think when we learn another language,
when we're reading, we're also sub

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:40.770
vocalizing, because when we read our
own language, it's instant, meaning

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:42.390
we don't need to sub vocalize.

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:45.750
We Don't need to say it to ourselves, but
when we're reading in a foreign language,

00:12:45.780 --> 00:12:50.810
I find that I'm saying it to myself, but
I very rarely read out loud, out loud.

00:12:50.949 --> 00:12:51.959
It's tiring.

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:54.165
But I tend to sub vocalize.

00:12:54.435 --> 00:12:57.945
And that's why combining listening
with reading is so important

00:12:58.095 --> 00:13:01.245
because it gives you that
momentum, it gives you that sense.

00:13:01.245 --> 00:13:05.555
It gives you greater confidence that
you are self vocalizing with the right

00:13:05.915 --> 00:13:07.835
pronunciation and the right intonation.

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:11.640
Person says I never thought
of listening while exercising.

00:13:11.970 --> 00:13:15.570
I mean, come on, you can listen
while you're doing anything.

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:18.420
Washing the dishes, driving
a car, exercising, working

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:19.530
in the garden, you name it.

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:21.930
Someone here says that
the voice is too low.

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:23.460
I've had this comment before.

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:24.720
I, I don't know.

00:13:24.810 --> 00:13:28.770
Uh, I think when I listened to
my videos, the audio sounds fine.

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:31.440
Please let me know if
the audio is a problem.

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:34.350
Here's what we call a soft question.

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:37.310
What is the best app or program
to learn a new language.

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:41.209
Well, of course, I'm going
to say LingQ L I N G Q.

00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:46.849
And finally here's one from Nikita
who says, uh, how do you go into

00:13:46.849 --> 00:13:49.550
self limited vocabulary in German?

00:13:49.880 --> 00:13:52.160
When I read interesting
stuff, I don't understand it.

00:13:52.330 --> 00:13:58.515
So this is a constant issue of, you know,
the beginner stuff is easy because the

00:13:58.515 --> 00:14:00.314
word frequency, declines very quickly.

00:14:00.314 --> 00:14:06.314
So in an initial period, you have contact
with a lot of high frequency words,

00:14:06.345 --> 00:14:11.295
like our mini stories, and then as you
move into natural content, of course,

00:14:11.295 --> 00:14:15.435
now all of a sudden you're dealing
with far more low frequency words.

00:14:15.555 --> 00:14:16.635
So what do you do?

00:14:16.665 --> 00:14:17.765
Well, it's a bit of a...

00:14:18.724 --> 00:14:22.864
I believe that that the lack
of intermediate content is

00:14:22.864 --> 00:14:24.425
a problem in many languages.

00:14:24.594 --> 00:14:27.354
I experienced it in Arabic and in Persian.

00:14:27.625 --> 00:14:30.385
That's where I'm grateful to
Sahra who has created a lot of

00:14:30.415 --> 00:14:32.574
excellent content in Persian.

00:14:32.875 --> 00:14:35.665
I've mentioned, you know, if
Français Authentique or Inner

00:14:35.665 --> 00:14:40.015
French, which are great sources
of intermediate content in French.

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:41.939
But it is a problem.

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:47.910
And, uh, I think at LingQ, for example,
if I can find content that has 10, 15%

00:14:47.910 --> 00:14:50.160
new words, that's a comfort zone there.

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:54.660
I'm learning a lot of new words because
some of those 10, 15% are in fact names.

00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:56.069
So it's actually less than that.

00:14:56.610 --> 00:14:58.890
And so that to me is good content.

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:02.520
I'm coming across new words,
and yet I have enough known

00:15:02.520 --> 00:15:04.260
words that it's not painful.

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:08.270
But I have gone through Russian
for example, in the early days

00:15:08.270 --> 00:15:11.300
when I was with Russian and LingQ
was much slower than it is now.

00:15:11.300 --> 00:15:17.210
And I had material with 40% new words,
and I just fought on because I wanted to

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:21.080
learn this language and I was interested
in whatever it was that I was reading.

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:24.620
It could have been, you know,
tolstoy or something, and I just

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:30.370
struggled on, so it's a function
of our, you know, Pain threshold.

00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:34.330
So, and, and I tend to find
myself bouncing back and forth.

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:37.180
I fight my way through a difficult
content, lots of new words.

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:38.830
And ah, I'm tired of doing that.

00:15:38.890 --> 00:15:42.790
I get back to my mini stories and this
kind of gives me a little more confidence.

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:45.280
And then I go back and fight
with the more difficult content.

00:15:45.490 --> 00:15:51.250
And you just have to find a proper mix
of easy content and difficult content

00:15:51.250 --> 00:15:53.260
so that you maintain your motivation.

00:15:53.590 --> 00:15:58.005
You get enough fluency becasue you're
dealing with easier content and yet

00:15:58.005 --> 00:16:01.365
you're acquiring new words and you're
fighting way fighting your way through

00:16:01.365 --> 00:16:05.385
to, uh, eventually getting enough
vocabulary so that you can deal with

00:16:05.685 --> 00:16:07.965
more authentic content more easily.

00:16:08.115 --> 00:16:08.625
Okay.

00:16:08.805 --> 00:16:10.485
I'm just going to deal
with those questions today.

00:16:10.485 --> 00:16:11.925
I hope that was of interest.

00:16:12.225 --> 00:16:13.665
I see I've gone on a bit longer.

00:16:13.995 --> 00:16:18.285
Um, leave you a few videos kind
of relevant related to this.

00:16:18.615 --> 00:16:20.685
And let me know if this
is of interest to you.

00:16:21.195 --> 00:16:21.795
Thank you.

00:16:22.155 --> 00:16:22.655
Bye for now.

